1
00:00:00,500 --> 00:00:03,871
You open CloudCart in your browser, and a page appears.

2
00:00:04,421 --> 00:00:08,026
It feels instant. But five different things just worked together.

3
00:00:08,576 --> 00:00:12,779
A client asked. A network carried the question. A server received it.

4
00:00:13,329 --> 00:00:16,785
An application did the work, on top of an operating system.

5
00:00:17,335 --> 00:00:21,815
Today you learn to tell these five apart, because every outage lives in one of them.

6
00:00:23,865 --> 00:00:25,038
First, the client.

7
00:00:25,588 --> 00:00:27,828
A client is whoever asks for something.

8
00:00:28,378 --> 00:00:29,872
Your browser is a client.

9
00:00:30,422 --> 00:00:31,915
A phone app is a client.

10
00:00:32,465 --> 00:00:34,769
A script that checks prices is a client.

11
00:00:35,319 --> 00:00:39,415
Notice: a client is not a person. It is software making a request.

12
00:00:39,965 --> 00:00:42,738
Being a client is a role, not a type of machine.

13
00:00:44,788 --> 00:00:45,940
Next, the server.

14
00:00:46,490 --> 00:00:48,837
A server is whoever answers requests.

15
00:00:49,387 --> 00:00:51,989
The word has two meanings, and people mix them up.

16
00:00:52,539 --> 00:00:54,843
It can mean a program that waits for requests.

17
00:00:55,393 --> 00:00:58,252
It can also mean the machine that runs such a program.

18
00:00:58,802 --> 00:01:01,810
Any computer can act as a server, even a laptop.

19
00:01:02,360 --> 00:01:05,837
What makes it a server is the job it does, not how big it is.

20
00:01:07,887 --> 00:01:09,210
Now, the application.

21
00:01:09,760 --> 00:01:13,706
An application is a program that does a useful job for people.

22
00:01:14,256 --> 00:01:18,971
CloudCart's shop code is an application: it lists products, and takes orders.

23
00:01:19,521 --> 00:01:24,236
A database program is an application too, because it stores and finds data.

24
00:01:24,786 --> 00:01:28,626
Applications decide what happens. But they cannot run on their own.

25
00:01:29,176 --> 00:01:30,754
They need something underneath.

26
00:01:32,804 --> 00:01:34,937
That something is the operating system.

27
00:01:35,487 --> 00:01:40,031
The operating system manages the machine, so applications do not have to.

28
00:01:40,581 --> 00:01:42,288
It starts and stops programs.

29
00:01:42,838 --> 00:01:45,547
It shares memory and processor time between them.

30
00:01:46,097 --> 00:01:48,871
It stores files, and controls who may open them.

31
00:01:49,421 --> 00:01:51,575
And it handles the connection to the network.

32
00:01:52,125 --> 00:01:55,560
Linux, Windows, and macOS are operating systems.

33
00:01:56,110 --> 00:01:58,329
Most servers you will manage run Linux.

34
00:02:00,379 --> 00:02:03,110
Put it together, and one machine is a stack of layers.

35
00:02:03,660 --> 00:02:06,945
At the bottom is hardware: processor, memory, and disks.

36
00:02:07,495 --> 00:02:09,308
Above it, the operating system.

37
00:02:09,858 --> 00:02:11,522
Above that, the applications.

38
00:02:12,072 --> 00:02:14,974
Your laptop has this stack, and so does the server.

39
00:02:15,524 --> 00:02:17,742
Between the two machines sits the network.

40
00:02:18,292 --> 00:02:21,620
The network is simply the connection that lets computers talk.

41
00:02:23,670 --> 00:02:27,169
Here is the idea that confuses beginners: roles change.

42
00:02:27,719 --> 00:02:31,665
Your browser asks the shop for a page. The browser is the client.

43
00:02:32,215 --> 00:02:36,546
The shop then asks the database for your order. Now the shop is the client.

44
00:02:37,096 --> 00:02:40,232
The database answers. The database is the server.

45
00:02:40,782 --> 00:02:45,369
So the shop application is a server to the browser, and a client of the database.

46
00:02:45,919 --> 00:02:49,567
Always ask: in this conversation, who asks, and who answers?

47
00:02:51,617 --> 00:02:54,390
Let us follow one request through all five parts.

48
00:02:54,940 --> 00:02:57,628
One: the browser, the client, sends the request.

49
00:02:58,178 --> 00:03:00,376
Two: the network carries it to the server.

50
00:03:00,926 --> 00:03:05,896
Three: the operating system on the server receives it, and hands it to the right program.

51
00:03:06,446 --> 00:03:11,310
Four: the shop application builds the page, asking the database when it needs data.

52
00:03:11,860 --> 00:03:14,826
Five: the database finds the data, and answers.

53
00:03:15,376 --> 00:03:18,938
Then the reply travels back the same way, and your page appears.

54
00:03:20,988 --> 00:03:24,615
Now let us see this for real, in a disposable Linux sandbox.

55
00:03:25,165 --> 00:03:28,365
These outputs are real captures, not an illustration.

56
00:03:28,915 --> 00:03:32,691
First command: the operating system reports that it is Linux.

57
00:03:33,241 --> 00:03:38,531
Second: a program is listening on port eight zero eight zero. That is a server, waiting.

58
00:03:39,081 --> 00:03:44,223
Third: a client, called curl, asks it for a page. The answer says two hundred, okay.

59
00:03:44,773 --> 00:03:47,290
Fourth: we ask a port where nothing is listening.

60
00:03:47,840 --> 00:03:51,275
No answer, and exit code seven: the connection failed.

61
00:03:51,825 --> 00:03:56,475
Same client, same network. The difference is whether a server is there to answer.

62
00:03:58,525 --> 00:04:00,381
Why does this matter for DevOps?

63
00:04:00,931 --> 00:04:02,702
Because a symptom points to a layer.

64
00:04:03,252 --> 00:04:07,433
Cannot connect at all? Suspect the network, or that nothing is listening.

65
00:04:07,983 --> 00:04:11,418
The page loads, but shows an error? Suspect the application.

66
00:04:11,968 --> 00:04:13,095
Everything is slow?

67
00:04:13,095 --> 00:04:17,365
Suspect the operating system or the hardware running short of resources.

68
00:04:17,915 --> 00:04:21,840
The server will not start? Look at the operating system, and the machine.

69
00:04:22,390 --> 00:04:24,822
Name the layer first, then investigate it.

70
00:04:26,872 --> 00:04:28,728
Watch out for four common myths.

71
00:04:29,278 --> 00:04:31,391
Myth one: a server is a special,

72
00:04:31,391 --> 00:04:32,250
big computer.

73
00:04:32,250 --> 00:04:32,778
In fact,

74
00:04:32,778 --> 00:04:35,486
it is any computer doing the serving job.

75
00:04:36,036 --> 00:04:40,045
Myth two: the operating system and the application are the same.

76
00:04:40,045 --> 00:04:40,546
In fact,

77
00:04:40,546 --> 00:04:41,924
one runs on the other.

78
00:04:42,474 --> 00:04:44,935
Myth three: the network is the internet.

79
00:04:44,935 --> 00:04:45,427
In fact,

80
00:04:45,427 --> 00:04:46,411
your home Wi-Fi,

81
00:04:46,411 --> 00:04:47,764
and an office network,

82
00:04:47,764 --> 00:04:48,810
are networks too.

83
00:04:49,360 --> 00:04:53,435
Myth four: a client is a person. In fact, it is software that asks.

84
00:04:53,985 --> 00:04:56,459
Clear these up, and later topics get easier.

85
00:04:58,509 --> 00:05:00,984
Here is how you can check your own understanding.

86
00:05:01,534 --> 00:05:04,456
Pick any website you use, and describe one visit.

87
00:05:05,006 --> 00:05:06,841
Name the client, and the server.

88
00:05:07,391 --> 00:05:10,058
Name the application, and the operating system.

89
00:05:10,608 --> 00:05:12,165
Show the network between them.

90
00:05:12,715 --> 00:05:15,958
Then pick one symptom, and name the layer you would check first.

91
00:05:16,508 --> 00:05:18,193
Check yourself on six points.

92
00:05:18,743 --> 00:05:20,364
To pass, you need all six.

93
00:05:22,414 --> 00:05:23,289
Your turn.

94
00:05:23,839 --> 00:05:26,185
Choose a site or an app you use every day.

95
00:05:26,735 --> 00:05:28,591
One: draw the five parts for it.

96
00:05:29,141 --> 00:05:32,576
Two: invent one failure, and name the layer it belongs to.

97
00:05:33,126 --> 00:05:35,131
Three: mark yourself on the rubric.

98
00:05:35,681 --> 00:05:37,409
Pause the video, and do it now.

99
00:05:50,959 --> 00:05:51,834
Quick check.

100
00:05:52,384 --> 00:05:54,965
What is the difference between a client and a server?

101
00:05:55,515 --> 00:05:57,328
What does an operating system do?

102
00:05:57,878 --> 00:05:59,926
Which layers sit inside one machine?

103
00:06:00,476 --> 00:06:02,652
Name the five parts of a web request.

104
00:06:03,202 --> 00:06:07,597
Objective covered: MC02-O01.

105
00:06:08,147 --> 00:06:12,072
Pause the video, and describe one site visit with all five parts.

106
